      <h1 class="hbs-h1">Hourglass Body Shape</h1>
      <p class="hbs-subtitle">Balanced bust and hips, a clearly defined waist — the rarest of the seven female body shapes, at roughly 8% of women. Here's exactly what defines it, how it differs from its close cousins, and how to dress for it.</p>
    </header>
  </div>

  <div class="hbs-art">

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-what">
      <h2>What Is an Hourglass Body Shape?</h2>
      <p>An hourglass body shape has a bust and hips that are close to equal in width, with a waist that is clearly, visibly narrower than both. It's the shape most people picture when they hear "curvy" — but the definition is stricter than that word suggests. Not to know your exact shape yet? Run your numbers through our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> first; the rest of this guide will make more sense once you have your own figures in front of you.</p>
      <p>In measurement terms, the difference between your bust and hips has to be under 3.6 inches, and your waist has to sit at least 9 inches smaller than your bust <em>or</em> at least 10 inches smaller than your hips. Both conditions matter — a body with balanced bust and hips but a soft, undefined waist is a rectangle, not an hourglass. The waist is what earns the name.</p>
      <div class="hbs-note">
        <p><strong>Quick check:</strong> bust no more than 1 inch bigger than hips, hips no more than 3.6 inches bigger than bust, waist 9+ inches smaller than the bust. If that's you, you're a true hourglass — not top hourglass, not bottom hourglass, the classic version.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-rare">
      <h2>How Rare Is the Hourglass Shape, Really?</h2>
      <p>The most-cited figure comes from a 2006 North Carolina State University analysis of more than 6,300 women, led by Dr. Cindy Istook, which found that only about 8% measured as a true hourglass — Rectangle came out far more common at roughly 46%, followed by pear at around 20% and apple at around 14%. That specific breakdown traces to a press release rather than a published table, and a 2021 re-analysis of the same formulas on 3D body scans found a notably higher hourglass share (26–34%) — so treat these as the most commonly repeated figures, not an exact census. The classification <em>formula</em> itself is separately documented in peer-reviewed clothing-science literature.</p>
      <p>That 8% figure surprises most people, because "hourglass" gets used loosely in everyday conversation to describe anyone with visible curves. The strict version — where bust and hips are within an inch of each other <em>and</em> the waist is dramatically smaller than both — is the rarest of the seven shapes, not the default "curvy" category.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-compare">
      <h2>Hourglass vs Top Hourglass vs Bottom Hourglass</h2>
      <p>These three get confused constantly because they share the same defined waist. The difference is which half is slightly larger — and it changes what actually flatters you.</p>
      <div class="hbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Trait</th><th scope="col">Hourglass</th><th scope="col">Top Hourglass</th><th scope="col">Bottom Hourglass</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Bust vs. hips</td><td>Within 1 in of each other</td><td>Bust 1–10 in larger</td><td>Hips 3.6–10 in larger</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waist</td><td>≥9 in smaller than bust</td><td>≥9 in smaller than bust</td><td>≥9 in smaller than hips</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Visual balance</td><td>Symmetrical</td><td>Fuller on top</td><td>Fuller below</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Styling focus</td><td>Show the waist, either half works</td><td>Necklines and tops carry the look</td><td>Bottoms and hemlines carry the look</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If you've tried classic hourglass styling advice and it hasn't quite landed, this table is usually why — you're likely a top or bottom hourglass, and the small shift in emphasis (upper body vs. lower body) makes a real difference in what looks intentional versus slightly off. Our full <a href="/">body type calculator</a> distinguishes all three using your exact numbers.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-whr">
      <h2>What's a Healthy Waist-to-Hip Ratio for This Shape?</h2>
      <p>Because the hourglass definition requires a waist well below the bust and hip measurements, the waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) for this shape usually lands between 0.65 and 0.75 — comfortably under the WHO's 0.85 threshold for elevated health risk in women. A defined waist and a low WHR tend to go together, though WHR is worth checking on its own rather than assumed from shape alone, since it uses your hip measurement specifically rather than bust.</p>
      <p>Get the exact number and what it means for you on our <a href="/waist-to-hip-ratio-calculator/">waist to hip ratio calculator</a> — it takes the same two measurements you've likely already got on hand.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-style">
      <h2>How to Dress for an Hourglass Body Shape</h2>
      <p>The core rule for this shape is simple and easy to forget: your proportions are already balanced, so clothing that <em>hides</em> the waist is working against you, not for you. Structure and stretch matter more than trend here.</p>

      <h3>Work and Formal</h3>
      <p>A wrap dress in a mid-weight fabric — not so stiff it stands away from the body, not so soft it clings everywhere — is close to a guaranteed win. Pencil skirts with a fitted blouse tucked in, or a belted sheath dress, do the same job. Look for darts and princess seams rather than a straight-cut boxy blazer, which erases the waist you're trying to show.</p>

      <h3>Casual and Everyday</h3>
      <p>High-waisted jeans with a tucked-in or cropped top keep the waistline visible without effort. A wrap top over straight-leg trousers works the same angle. Avoid oversized boyfriend shirts worn untucked — on this shape specifically, that silhouette reads as bigger than it is, because there's no waist reference point left for the eye to anchor on.</p>

      <h3>Special Occasions</h3>
      <p>Mermaid and fit-and-flare gowns are built almost exactly around hourglass proportions, which is why they photograph so well on this shape. A corseted or boned bodice with a flowing skirt does the same thing in a softer way. Skip empire waistlines here — they're designed to hide a waist, and yours is the feature, not the flaw.</p>

      <h3>Swimwear</h3>
      <p>A high-waisted bottom with a underwire or shelf-bra top keeps the natural waist as the visual center. Cut-out one-pieces at the waist do the same job in a single garment. Avoid low-rise bottoms paired with a triangle top — together they flatten the exact contrast that makes this shape distinctive.</p>

      <h3>What to Avoid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Drop-waist dresses, which shift the visual waistline down to your natural hip and cancel out your defined middle</li>
        <li>Boxy, unstructured blazers with no waist seam</li>
        <li>Fabric with zero stretch in fitted styles — it fights the curve instead of following it</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-measure">
      <h2>Common Hourglass Measurements by Size</h2>
      <p>The formula holds at every size — it's about the relationship between your numbers, not the numbers themselves. Here's what a true hourglass looks like across a petite, average, and plus-size frame:</p>
      <div class="hbs-tbl-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Frame</th><th scope="col">Bust</th><th scope="col">Waist</th><th scope="col">Hips</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Petite</td><td>34 in</td><td>25 in</td><td>35 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Average</td><td>38 in</td><td>28 in</td><td>39 in</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Plus-size</td><td>46 in</td><td>36 in</td><td>47 in</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Notice the pattern in each row: bust and hips sit within an inch of each other, and the waist is a full 9–10 inches smaller than both. That gap is what a stylist means by "defined waist" — it's a specific, measurable thing, not a vague impression.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="hbs-sec" id="hbs-faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>What is the exact ratio for an hourglass body shape?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>There's no single "hourglass ratio" number, because the shape is defined by differences in inches rather than a percentage. The two conditions are: bust no more than 1 inch bigger than hips (hips can be up to 3.6 inches bigger than bust), and the waist at least 9 inches smaller than the bust (or 10 inches smaller than the hips). Translated to WHR, that usually works out to roughly 0.65–0.75.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>Is the hourglass body shape actually rare?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>Yes, by the most widely cited figures — a 2006 NC State analysis puts it around 8% of women, the least common of the seven shapes in that data, well behind rectangle (46%), pear (20%) and apple (14%). It's talked about as though it's the default "curvy" shape, but the strict measurement definition is genuinely uncommon. Worth knowing: those exact percentages come from a press release rather than a peer-reviewed table, and other re-analyses have found different splits — the general pattern (hourglass is rare, rectangle is common) holds up better than any single precise number.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>What's the difference between hourglass and bottom hourglass?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>Both have a clearly defined waist. In a true hourglass, bust is no more than 1 inch bigger than hips (hips can run up to 3.6 inches bigger than bust). In a bottom hourglass, the hips are 3.6 to 10 inches larger than the bust — still a defined waist, just with the fullness sitting lower. Four-shape systems usually lump bottom hourglass in with "pear," but pear shapes don't have the same sharp waist definition.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>Can exercise change your body into an hourglass shape?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>Not fully — bust and hip width are largely set by your skeletal frame and breast tissue, which training doesn't change. What resistance training <em>can</em> do is build the muscles around your shoulders, back and glutes, which shifts the visual proportion of an existing shape and can nudge a rectangle or triangle frame toward reading as more balanced. It won't convert your skeleton into a different category, but it does change what you see in the mirror.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>What is a "soft" hourglass?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>"Soft hourglass" is a styling-world term (not part of the seven-shape measurement system) for someone who is close to hourglass proportions but with slightly less dramatic waist definition, or a bit more overall softness to the frame. If your numbers land just outside the strict 9-inch threshold, you're likely in this territory — most of the same styling advice still applies.</p>
      </div></details>

      <details class="hbs-faq"><summary>Does the hourglass shape change with age or pregnancy?</summary><div class="hbs-faq-body">
        <p>Yes. Pregnancy changes waist and hip measurements temporarily and sometimes permanently; menopause-related hormonal shifts often move fat storage toward the abdomen, which can soften a previously sharp waist definition over time. It's worth re-measuring every six months or after a major life change rather than treating one result as fixed for life.</p>
      </div></details>
    </section>

    <p>Most of the confusion around this shape comes from treating "hourglass" as a general compliment rather than a specific measurement outcome — once you've got your actual bust, waist and hip numbers next to the formula above, it stops being a matter of opinion.</p>

